<span id="S4.T2.3.11.3.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.T2.3.11.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">(2)</span><span id="S4.T2.3.11.3.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> - SSH: 22, HTTP: 80</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.3.11.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_b ltx_border_t">
<span id="S4.T2.3.11.4.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:241.9pt;">
<span id="S4.T2.3.11.4.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.T2.3.11.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">(6)</span><span id="S4.T2.3.11.4.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> - Port scanning </span><span id="S4.T2.3.11.4.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">-&gt;</span><span id="S4.T2.3.11.4.1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> Web enumeration </span><span id="S4.T2.3.11.4.1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">-&gt;</span><span id="S4.T2.3.11.4.1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> Command injection </span><span id="S4.T2.3.11.4.1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">-&gt;</span><span id="S4.T2.3.11.4.1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> File enumeration </span><span id="S4.T2.3.11.4.1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">-&gt;</span><span id="S4.T2.3.11.4.1.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> Shell construction </span><span id="S4.T2.3.11.4.1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">-&gt;</span><span id="S4.T2.3.11.4.1.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> Code analysis</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS3.4" class="ltx_text">IV-C</span> </span><span id="S4.SS3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Performance Metrics</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate performance using quantitative and qualitative metrics.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Quantitative analysis:</span> We use the following metrics to measure a tool’s performance quantitatively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Subtask Completion % (<math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math>)</span> – The percentage of subtask completion for each machine. These subtasks were manually extracted from the official write-ups for each machine, available on the HTB platform. The subtask breakdown is given in Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="TABLE II ‣ IV-B PentestGPT Baseline ‣ IV Experiment Setup ‣ AutoPentester: An LLM Agent-based Framework for Automated Pentesting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Services Coverage % (<math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math>)</span> – The number of services (such as HTTP, FTP, and SSH) covered during the pentesting process.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">No. of Steps (<math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math>)</span> – The number of steps used for each machine. A step refers to a complete iteration in the tool as illustrated in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ I Introduction ‣ AutoPentester: An LLM Agent-based Framework for Automated Pentesting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">No of. Loops (<math id="S4.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math>)</span> – The number of repetitive steps where the tool tries the same step repeatedly.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Human Interaction (<math id="S4.I1.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math>)</span> – The number of steps requiring human intervention, including:</p>
<ul id="S4.I1.i5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I1.i5.I1.i1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Running commands on a security tool (e.g., Metasploit) and reporting results to the agent.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I1.i5.I1.i2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Adjusting or correcting incomplete commands.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I1.i5.I1.i3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Observing visual results (e.g., in web applications) and interpreting them in text format for the tool.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">No. of Incomplete Commands (<math id="S4.I1.i6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math>)</span> – The number of incomplete or erroneous commands generated by the tool.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Vulnerability Coverage (<math id="S4.I1.i7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math>)</span> – The number of vulnerabilities covered during the pentesting process. Note that this metric is used only with the custom VM experiments where we know the exact number of existing vulnerabilities.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We carefully analyzed the log files generated on each machine during the experiments and manually counted the aforementioned metrics. Subtask completion was determined by verifying if a subtask’s requirements, as detailed in the official write-ups, were met. For instance, if a website contained specific information accessible via a vulnerable endpoint, the subtask was considered complete once that endpoint was identified and the information extracted. To minimize human error, two authors independently counted and cross-verified these results. We run experiments three times for each target machine and report average values
in Section <a href="#S5.SS1" title="V-A Quantitative Analysis ‣ V Results ‣ AutoPentester: An LLM Agent-based Framework for Automated Pentesting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V-A</span></a>.
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Qualitative analysis:</span> We recruited 10 cybersecurity professional volunteers with more than five years of experience via a LinkedIn post. The participants included seven pentesters and three cybersecurity professionals, all actively working in the field. This user study was approved by the Human Research Ethics Committee of The University of Sydney under the application number 2024/HE001529.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We generated pentest reports using PentestGPT and AutoPentester for two Hack The Box (HTB) machines (Active and Bashed) and two virtual machines (VM1 and VM4). Industry experts were then asked to complete a questionnaire based on their evaluations. Additionally, we provided screen recordings of the tools to give insight into their functionality. The questionnaire consists of 13 MCQs and 15 short-answer questions. The questions on HTB machines reports were more towards attack strategies and the maturity of the steps and commands of the tools, while the questions on VMs were towards the vulnerability coverage and quality of reporting. However, both HTB machines and VMs shared four common questions regarding attack surface coverage (Q1), the extent of disclosed information (Q2), the progression of attack steps (Q3), and the efficiency of those steps (Q5). The questions and survey results are given in Section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="V-B Qualitative Analysis ‣ V Results ‣ AutoPentester: An LLM Agent-based Framework for Automated Pentesting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V-B</span></a>. We have uploaded the experiment log files, questionnaire, and responses to our GitHub repository.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS4.4" class="ltx_text">IV-D</span> </span><span id="S4.SS4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Choice of LLM</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">AutoPentester can be integrated into any LLM that provides an API. Different LLMs will have different knowledge levels on cybersecurity-related tasks depending on their training data. Therefore, they may perform differently on pentesting tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To pick the best LLM backend for AutoPentester, we first tested AutoPentester using the three LLMs; Gemini-2.0-flash, GPT-3.5-turbo, and GPT-4-turbo on HTB machines to test their ability to understand the attack environment (<span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">cf.</span> Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="TABLE III ‣ IV-D Choice of LLM ‣ IV Experiment Setup ‣ AutoPentester: An LLM Agent-based Framework for Automated Pentesting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a>). We selected the LLM with the highest subtask completion percentage, which was GPT-4-turbo, as the backbone for the rest of the experiments.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE III: </span>Performance comparison of different LLMs. Here (L) represents Linux and (W) represents Windows.</figcaption>
<table id="S4.T3.3" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T3.3.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T3.3.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:-0.45pt;padding-bottom:-0.45pt;"><span id="S4.T3.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Name</span></th>
<th id="S4.T3.3.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:-0.45pt;padding-bottom:-0.45pt;"><span id="S4.T3.3.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Level</span></th>
<th id="S4.T3.3.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:-0.45pt;padding-bottom:-0.45pt;" colspan="3"><span id="S4.T3.3.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Subtask Completion (%)</span></th></tr>
<tr id="S4.T3.3.2" class="ltx_tr">
